1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,612
Over eight episodes,
you've learned to draw,

2
00:00:02,612 --> 00:00:08,359
animate, write equations, plot graphs and
fly a camera through three dimensions.

3
00:00:08,359 --> 00:00:11,436
You've probably made a few
things of your own along the way.

4
00:00:11,436 --> 00:00:14,234
So: now what? How do you keep your work,

5
00:00:14,234 --> 00:00:16,777
turn it into a real video file,

6
00:00:16,777 --> 00:00:20,561
show it to a friend, or find
inspiration for the next one?

7
00:00:20,561 --> 00:00:24,625
This episode is a practical
tour of ManimOnline itself,

8
00:00:24,625 --> 00:00:26,471
from the first click to the finished,

9
00:00:26,471 --> 00:00:28,700
shareable thing.

10
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:32,996
The quickest way to learn what's possible
is to look at what others have made.

11
00:00:32,996 --> 00:00:35,260
The Sample gallery opens in a new tab,

12
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:37,129
so your current work stays put.

13
00:00:37,129 --> 00:00:38,998
Each card has a cover picture,

14
00:00:38,998 --> 00:00:41,796
and animated samples have
a Play preview button,

15
00:00:41,796 --> 00:00:43,898
which plays a short pre-rendered video,

16
00:00:43,898 --> 00:00:45,895
without running any Python at all.

17
00:00:45,895 --> 00:00:48,530
You can search by title,
and filter by language.

18
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:50,144
When something catches your eye,

19
00:00:50,144 --> 00:00:51,734
choose Load in editor.

20
00:00:51,734 --> 00:00:56,030
The source opens and renders, ready
for you to pull apart and change.

21
00:00:56,030 --> 00:00:59,084
And the editor remembers your
edits in the page address,

22
00:00:59,084 --> 00:01:01,301
so reloading the page keeps your version,

23
00:01:01,301 --> 00:01:03,869
not the original.

24
00:01:04,167 --> 00:01:06,292
A single file can hold several scenes.

25
00:01:06,292 --> 00:01:09,183
That's handy for keeping the
parts of a longer video together,

26
00:01:09,183 --> 00:01:10,831
sharing helper functions between them.

27
00:01:10,831 --> 00:01:13,873
The Scene field in the toolbar
chooses which one to render.

28
00:01:13,873 --> 00:01:16,532
Leave it empty, and you get
the first scene in the file.

29
00:01:16,532 --> 00:01:20,142
Start typing, and it suggests the scene
names it found the last time the file

30
00:01:20,142 --> 00:01:20,630
rendered.

31
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:22,708
This code opens with Middle selected,

32
00:01:22,708 --> 00:01:25,692
because the scene choice is
saved along with the source.

33
00:01:25,692 --> 00:01:28,887
Try switching to Intro and Outro.

34
00:01:29,817 --> 00:01:33,672
You have probably noticed that
ManimOnline renders by itself.

35
00:01:33,672 --> 00:01:35,692
When you stop typing for about a second,

36
00:01:35,692 --> 00:01:37,967
it starts. If you don't want to wait,

37
00:01:37,967 --> 00:01:40,753
the Render animation
button starts immediately,

38
00:01:40,753 --> 00:01:43,714
and it's also how you
retry after an error.

39
00:01:43,714 --> 00:01:45,978
But what if your code never finishes?

40
00:01:45,978 --> 00:01:48,776
Programs can loop forever,
and this one does,

41
00:01:48,776 --> 00:01:51,040
on purpose. Scan it if you like.

42
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:53,431
The editor stays perfectly responsive,

43
00:01:53,431 --> 00:01:56,357
because the Python runs
separately from the page.

44
00:01:56,357 --> 00:02:00,537
Press Cancel render, and that
separate process is simply stopped.

45
00:02:00,537 --> 00:02:04,612
If you don't, the editor stops
it for you after ninety seconds.

46
00:02:04,612 --> 00:02:07,050
There are a few other
limits worth knowing.

47
00:02:07,050 --> 00:02:09,547
Previews run at fifteen frames per second,

48
00:02:09,547 --> 00:02:10,986
to keep rendering fast.

49
00:02:10,986 --> 00:02:14,446
A single preview can be up to a
hundred and twenty seconds long.

50
00:02:14,446 --> 00:02:17,395
For longer videos, split
them into several scenes.

51
00:02:17,395 --> 00:02:20,599
Episode ten explains
why these limits exist,

52
00:02:20,599 --> 00:02:24,017
and how that "separate
process" actually works.

53
00:02:24,017 --> 00:02:27,686
To keep what you've made, use the
Export menu next to the Download button.

54
00:02:27,686 --> 00:02:31,006
S V G frame saves the current
frame as a vector image,

55
00:02:31,006 --> 00:02:32,841
which stays sharp at any size.

56
00:02:32,841 --> 00:02:35,998
It's perfect for slides,
posters or a blog post.

57
00:02:35,998 --> 00:02:39,330
P N G frame saves the current
frame as an ordinary picture.

58
00:02:39,330 --> 00:02:42,999
MP4 and WebM save the whole
animation as a video file.

59
00:02:42,999 --> 00:02:45,298
The video is encoded right
there in your browser,

60
00:02:45,298 --> 00:02:47,201
from the frames it already computed.

61
00:02:47,201 --> 00:02:48,722
Nothing is uploaded anywhere.

62
00:02:48,722 --> 00:02:51,033
This needs a browser with
built-in video encoding,

63
00:02:51,033 --> 00:02:53,250
which recent versions of
the major browsers have,

64
00:02:53,250 --> 00:02:55,247
and if yours doesn't,
the editor tells you.

65
00:02:55,247 --> 00:02:58,497
Keep in mind that these videos have
the same frame rate as the preview,

66
00:02:58,497 --> 00:02:59,995
fifteen frames per second.

67
00:02:59,995 --> 00:03:01,771
That's great for sharing an idea quickly.

68
00:03:01,771 --> 00:03:04,326
For a polished,
sixty-frames-per-second production,

69
00:03:04,326 --> 00:03:05,765
which is how this series is made,

70
00:03:05,765 --> 00:03:09,067
you'll want full Manim, and
we'll come to that in a moment.

71
00:03:09,067 --> 00:03:12,295
The quickest way to show someone
your work is the Share button.

72
00:03:12,295 --> 00:03:15,093
It produces a link, and
here's the surprising part:

73
00:03:15,093 --> 00:03:17,345
that link contains your entire program.

74
00:03:17,345 --> 00:03:19,968
The code is compressed, and
tucked into the address itself,

75
00:03:19,968 --> 00:03:22,383
along with your scene choice
and renderer settings.

76
00:03:22,383 --> 00:03:25,715
Anyone who opens it sees
exactly what you saw.

77
00:03:25,715 --> 00:03:27,294
Because the code is inside the link,

78
00:03:27,294 --> 00:03:28,711
nothing is stored on a server,

79
00:03:28,711 --> 00:03:29,988
and it can never go missing.

80
00:03:29,988 --> 00:03:30,975
The price is length:

81
00:03:30,975 --> 00:03:33,239
bigger programs make longer links.

82
00:03:33,239 --> 00:03:37,593
Shorten link asks a public link-shortening
service for a compact version,

83
00:03:37,593 --> 00:03:39,137
which is handy for chat messages,

84
00:03:39,137 --> 00:03:41,598
though it depends on that
service being available.

85
00:03:41,598 --> 00:03:43,316
If you keep editing after you share,

86
00:03:43,316 --> 00:03:45,244
the old link still holds the old version,

87
00:03:45,244 --> 00:03:47,900
so share again when you're done.

88
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:51,673
For a short, permanent address
that doesn't grow with your code,

89
00:03:51,673 --> 00:03:56,015
there's HashBin. Save to
HashBin stores your source,

90
00:03:56,015 --> 00:03:57,919
together with your scene choice,

91
00:03:57,919 --> 00:04:00,740
and gives back a content identifier,

92
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:04,804
a C I D, which becomes the
end of the page's address.

93
00:04:04,804 --> 00:04:07,335
HashBin is a paid storage service,

94
00:04:07,335 --> 00:04:09,669
so the first time you'll
connect an account,

95
00:04:09,669 --> 00:04:11,631
and before anything is saved,

96
00:04:11,631 --> 00:04:15,729
the editor shows you the price and
how long the file will be kept.

97
00:04:15,729 --> 00:04:18,957
Loading saved work is
free, and anyone can do it:

98
00:04:18,957 --> 00:04:23,311
paste a C I D or a saved link
into the box beside the button,

99
00:04:23,311 --> 00:04:24,948
or simply open the link.

100
00:04:24,948 --> 00:04:29,011
And now you know what's inside
every QR code in this series.

101
00:04:29,011 --> 00:04:31,194
Each one is a HashBin link.

102
00:04:31,194 --> 00:04:34,213
The content identifier
isn't a random number:

103
00:04:34,213 --> 00:04:36,697
it's computed from the code itself,

104
00:04:36,697 --> 00:04:41,806
in a way that means it can only
ever refer to that exact code.

105
00:04:41,806 --> 00:04:44,789
Episode twelve shows how that works,

106
00:04:44,789 --> 00:04:51,800
and why it means a QR code can't be
quietly changed to point somewhere else.

107
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:53,090
After your first visit,

108
00:04:53,090 --> 00:04:56,572
ManimOnline quietly saves
everything it needs on your device:

109
00:04:56,572 --> 00:04:59,451
the editor, the gallery,
and the Python runtime.

110
00:04:59,451 --> 00:05:02,644
When that's finished, the
toolbar says Ready offline.

111
00:05:02,644 --> 00:05:03,515
From then on,

112
00:05:03,515 --> 00:05:07,439
you can open the page and render scenes
with no internet connection at all,

113
00:05:07,439 --> 00:05:11,560
on a plane, say. Only the things
that genuinely need the internet,

114
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:14,068
like saving to HashBin
or shortening links,

115
00:05:14,068 --> 00:05:16,933
wait until you're back online.

116
00:05:16,933 --> 00:05:20,161
ManimOnline started life
as an editor for diagrams,

117
00:05:20,161 --> 00:05:23,731
and it still speaks two diagram
languages alongside Manim.

118
00:05:23,731 --> 00:05:25,350
Graphviz describes graphs:

119
00:05:25,350 --> 00:05:27,127
boxes, and arrows between them.

120
00:05:27,127 --> 00:05:28,566
You say what connects to what,

121
00:05:28,566 --> 00:05:30,702
and Graphviz works out the layout.

122
00:05:30,702 --> 00:05:34,440
This diagram, fittingly, is
ManimOnline's own pipeline.

123
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:37,210
The Engine menu chooses
different layout styles.

124
00:05:37,210 --> 00:05:39,050
modl is a lighter language:

125
00:05:39,050 --> 00:05:41,383
one line per node or arrow, with shapes,

126
00:05:41,383 --> 00:05:42,417
colours and labels.

127
00:05:42,417 --> 00:05:46,051
Its diagrams are interactive, so
you can drag nodes into place,

128
00:05:46,051 --> 00:05:48,082
and the positions are
saved with your work.

129
00:05:48,082 --> 00:05:50,985
You never have to tell ManimOnline
which language you're writing.

130
00:05:50,985 --> 00:05:53,365
It reads the code, recognises the style,

131
00:05:53,365 --> 00:05:55,083
and picks the right renderer.

132
00:05:55,083 --> 00:05:59,050
If it guesses wrong, the
Renderer menu lets you choose.

133
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:04,704
A few habits make a big difference once
your scenes grow beyond a few lines.

134
00:06:04,704 --> 00:06:06,875
Build in small steps.

135
00:06:06,875 --> 00:06:09,812
Add one thing, let it
render, and check it,

136
00:06:09,812 --> 00:06:11,438
before adding the next.

137
00:06:11,438 --> 00:06:14,910
When something breaks, you'll
know exactly what broke it.

138
00:06:14,910 --> 00:06:18,671
Because ManimOnline
re-renders as you type,

139
00:06:18,671 --> 00:06:21,005
this rhythm comes almost for free.

140
00:06:21,005 --> 00:06:22,828
Give names to things you repeat.

141
00:06:22,828 --> 00:06:24,987
If you draw three labelled dots,

142
00:06:24,987 --> 00:06:27,472
write a little function
that makes a labelled dot.

143
00:06:27,472 --> 00:06:29,213
If x is always blue,

144
00:06:29,213 --> 00:06:33,532
define a constant for that colour at the
top of the file and use it everywhere.

145
00:06:33,532 --> 00:06:35,970
Then changing your mind
is a one-line edit.

146
00:06:35,970 --> 00:06:38,687
Prefer several short
scenes to one long one.

147
00:06:38,687 --> 00:06:41,404
They render faster, they
stay under the preview limit,

148
00:06:41,404 --> 00:06:43,564
and you can perfect each part on its own.

149
00:06:43,564 --> 00:06:44,934
And keep checkpoints.

150
00:06:44,934 --> 00:06:47,128
A share link, or a HashBin save,

151
00:06:47,128 --> 00:06:49,160
is a snapshot of your work at that moment.

152
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:50,704
Make one whenever something works,

153
00:06:50,704 --> 00:06:53,067
and you can always go back.

154
00:06:53,067 --> 00:06:56,318
At some point, you may want
full Manim on your own computer:

155
00:06:56,318 --> 00:06:59,104
for sixty-frame-per-second,
high-resolution video,

156
00:06:59,104 --> 00:07:03,574
for very long scenes, or for
features ManimOnline doesn't support.

157
00:07:03,574 --> 00:07:07,080
The Manim Community documentation
walks you through installing it.

158
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:09,147
Then you run your scene from a terminal:

159
00:07:09,147 --> 00:07:11,121
manim, a quality flag, your file,

160
00:07:11,121 --> 00:07:12,398
and the scene's name.

161
00:07:12,398 --> 00:07:14,662
Your code moves across almost unchanged,

162
00:07:14,662 --> 00:07:18,574
because ManimOnline's goal is to behave
like Manim Community for everything it

163
00:07:18,574 --> 00:07:20,037
supports.

164
00:07:20,037 --> 00:07:21,871
But be clear about the direction.

165
00:07:21,871 --> 00:07:24,635
ManimOnline runs a compatibility subset,

166
00:07:24,635 --> 00:07:29,604
not all of Manim. Most of what you write
in ManimOnline should run in Manim.

167
00:07:29,604 --> 00:07:32,297
Not everything written for
Manim will run in ManimOnline,

168
00:07:32,297 --> 00:07:34,950
and when it can't, it says so.

169
00:07:34,950 --> 00:07:36,355
Gallery for inspiration.

170
00:07:36,355 --> 00:07:37,806
Scenes chosen by name.

171
00:07:37,806 --> 00:07:39,559
Rendering that you can always cancel.

172
00:07:39,559 --> 00:07:41,150
Exports as pictures and videos,

173
00:07:41,150 --> 00:07:43,657
links that carry your code,
and HashBin for permanent,

174
00:07:43,657 --> 00:07:45,945
short addresses. Offline
whenever you need it,

175
00:07:45,945 --> 00:07:47,257
with diagrams on the side.

176
00:07:47,257 --> 00:07:48,812
That's the end of the main track.

177
00:07:48,812 --> 00:07:51,274
You now know enough to
make real explanations.

178
00:07:51,274 --> 00:07:54,582
If you've ever wondered how a
website runs Python with no server,

179
00:07:54,582 --> 00:07:58,379
or how you'd even check that a homemade
animation engine behaves like the real

180
00:07:58,379 --> 00:08:00,987
one, the next three
episodes open the hood.
